<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polygnotus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 475–447 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polygnotus was a Greek painter from Thasos, active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in the mid-5th century BCE. He painted large murals of mythical and historical scenes, among them the Sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> and the Underworld in the Lesche of the Knidians at Delphi, and works in the Stoa Poikile at Athens. Ancient writers praised the character and expression of his figures; none of his works survives.</p></div>
